While both Wireshark and Burp Suite are premier tools for capturing and analyzing network traffic on Ubuntu, they operate at completely different layers of the network.
The main takeaway is that Wireshark is a low-level packet analyzer used to see everything traveling across a network card, while Burp Suite is an application-layer proxy used specifically to inspect and modify web traffic (HTTP/HTTPS).
Here is a breakdown of exactly when to use each tool:

Comparison Overview


Feature	Wireshark	Burp Suite
Primary Focus	Entire network infrastructure and protocols.	Web applications and API security.
OSI Layer	Layers 2 through 7 (Data Link to Application).	Layer 7 only (Application layer).
Traffic Type	TCP, UDP, DNS, ICMP, DHCP, Wi-Fi management, etc.	Strictly HTTP and HTTPS (Web/Websockets).
Modification	Passive only (Read-only observation).	Active (Can intercept, edit, and replay traffic).
Encryption	Hard to read if traffic is encrypted (HTTPS looks like gibberish unless you manually supply keys).	Automatically decrypts HTTPS traffic by installing a local certificate.


When to Use Wireshark

You should use Wireshark when you need a "birds-eye view" of your network hardware or need to troubleshoot non-web protocols.
* Troubleshooting Network Connections: Figuring out why a machine isn't getting an IP address via DHCP, or checking if DNS queries are failing.
* Analyzing Wi-Fi Overhead: Inspecting low-level Wi-Fi data like beacon frames, handshakes, or signal strengths.
* Detecting Invisible Traffic: Finding background traffic generated by your operating system, smart devices, or malware that doesn't go through a standard web browser.
* Analyzing Non-Web Protocols: Inspecting database connections (SQL), file transfers (FTP/SMB), or streaming data (VoIP/RTP).
Example Scenario: Use Wireshark if your Ubuntu machine connects to a Wi-Fi network but fails to load the login page at all, and you need to check if the router is even assigning you an IP address.

When to Use Burp Suite

You should use Burp Suite when you are dealing specifically with a website, web app, or API, and you want to look closely at what data is being sent back and forth.
* Inspecting Web Forms: Seeing exactly what parameters (like usernames, passwords, or tokens) a login page sends to a server when you click "Submit".
* Manipulating Web Requests: Pausing a request mid-transit, changing a value (like changing a price from $100 to $1), and sending it to the server to see how the server responds.
* Analyzing Encrypted Web Traffic: Because Burp acts as a local proxy, it naturally handles SSL/TLS decryption, making HTTPS traffic perfectly readable.
* Testing Web APIs: Inspecting JSON or XML data being sent by mobile apps or modern web interfaces to backend servers.
Example Scenario: Use Burp Suite if the Wi-Fi captive portal page has successfully loaded, and you want to analyze how the login form formats your credentials before sending them to the authentication server.
Are you trying to fix a connectivity bug where the network won't route your traffic (better for Wireshark), or are you analyzing how a specific web page handles data inputs (better for Burp Suite)?
